The Universal Access to Quality Tertiary Education Act (UAQTE) or RA 10931 was signed  into law by President Rodrigo Roa Duterte on August 3, 2017, to make quality education in both higher and technical-vocational education and training more accessible to all Filipino students. It expanded and enhanced the coverage of the previously enacted Free Tuition 2017 which made tuition fees free in 112 State Universities and Colleges (SUCs) for Academic Year 2017-2018.

As of the present, the UAQTE is composed of four (4) equally important programs and implemented by the UniFAST, together with the CHED and the TESDA:

1. Free Higher Education (FHE) or free tuition and other school fees in SUCs and CHED-recognized Local Universities and Colleges (LUCs);
2. Tertiary Education Subsidy (TES) to support the full or partial cost of tertiary education of priority students in SUCs, CHED-recognized LUCs, and private Higher Education Institutions (HEIs);
3. Free Technical Vocational Education and Training (Free TVET) in state-run Technical Vocational Institutions (STVIs) which is implemented by TESDA; and
4. Student Loan Program (SLP) for undergraduate and graduate students in SUCs, CHED-recognized LUCs, and private HEIs (currently under review)

The realization of this project will provide significant support to poor but college-ready and academically excellent students by offering safe, modern, and conducive housing facilities. The proposed dormitory will feature a five-storey structure with a total floor area of 3,413.05 sq.m., distributed as follows:

Ground Floor – 810.70 sq.m.;
Second Floor – 649.95 sq.m.;
Third Floor – 650.80 sq.m.;
Fourth Floor – 650.80 sq.m.; and
Fifth Floor – 650.80 sq.m.

Designed to meet the highest standards of student welfare, the building will be equipped with a Scenic View Elevator, a complete electrical system, and modern lighting and fixtures.

It will also integrate advanced ICT infrastructure, including fiber optics trunk, CAT 6 networks, CCTV, telephone wiring, and a Public Address and Background Music (PABGM) system, alongside air-conditioning units and comprehensive fire protection services. To ensure uninterrupted operations, the facility will house an emergency generator with an overhead distribution transformer (3 × 150KW) and complete accessories. Altogether, this project will create a safe, inclusive, and future-ready environment that enhances student welfare and supports the University’s mission of academic excellence and holistic development.

The academic building shall house the following offices:
a)	Dean’s Office with a lounge area for faculty members and guests;
b)	Three (3) faculty rooms for the existing three (3) departments;
c)	One (1) conference room;
d)	Two (2) defense room;
e)	One (1) state-of-the-art computer laboratory;
f)	One (1) product development laboratory room; and
g)	Sixteen (16) classrooms with complete multi-media/ICT facilities.
           All offices and two (2) laboratories shall be fully-airconditioned. The said offices shall be composed of the dean’s office, faculty rooms of the three (3) departments, conference room and a separate two (2) defense room. The offices as faculty rooms shall be designed with cubicles, pantry and archive room. This shall be intended to accommodate twenty (20) Agribusiness faculty, five (5) BPA and BSAIS faculty respectively. While, there shall be one (1) laboratory room intended for Food and Non-Food Product Development activities designed to accommodate approximately 40-50 students enrolled in the course. Additionally, there shall be one (1) state-of-the-art computer laboratory that would serve also as lecture room for the conduct of online classes.
          While, all the classrooms shall be well-lighted and fully-ventilated. It shall also have an atrium, a space where kiosks can be positioned for the marketing activities of the business students. 
         Moreover, since Davao del Sur was heavily hit by three (3) strong earthquakes in 2019, and can still be felt until at present, the proposed building should also be earthquake resistant.

The SMART Digital Innovation Program – Tomas Oppus Campus (SMART-DIP TOC) is a multi-component infrastructure and academic modernization initiative designed to expand Southern Leyte State University – Tomas Oppus Campus’ capacity for digital learning, ICT-driven research, innovation development, and entrepreneurial training. The program addresses critical gaps in instructional space, outdated computer laboratories, insufficient digital infrastructure, and the absence of dedicated facilities for cultural engagement and student-led enterprise development. Through the construction of three major facilities—the Academic Building, ICT Building, and the Open Theater and Business Center—SMART-DIP TOC strengthens the campus’s ability to deliver outcomes-based, industry-aligned, and technology-enabled education for a rapidly growing student population.

1. Construction of Academic Building at Tomas Oppus Campus

This sub-project involves the construction and full equipping of a modern academic facility intended to address severe classroom shortages and the inadequacy of specialized teaching spaces. The building will include lecture rooms, simulation and demonstration rooms, educational technology laboratories, function rooms, plenary halls, faculty offices, a cafeteria, and VIP accommodations for visiting faculty and exchange partners. It will support teacher education, higher education instruction, research productivity, and internationalization activities. Equipped with interactive technologies and controlled internet connectivity, the facility strengthens the university’s ability to deliver blended and digital instruction consistent with CHED outcomes-based education reforms. Its construction also contributes to PDP-RM indicators related to improved learning environments, digital readiness of HEIs, and increased research and innovation outputs.

2. Construction of  ICT Building

This sub-project creates a purpose-built infrastructure specifically designed to upgrade ICT instruction, research capabilities, and technological innovation on campus. The building will house lecture rooms, collaboration and training halls, faculty offices, study spaces, a Data and Internet Connectivity Center, advanced computer laboratories, networking and cybersecurity laboratories, and a full Data Center for large-scale computing and storage needs. These facilities will be equipped with high-performance hardware, licensed software, VR/AR systems, and programmable technologies. The building directly addresses current development gaps such as outdated computer laboratories, insufficient ICT research spaces, and the lack of an institutional data hub—thereby enabling digital transformation, industry alignment, and enhanced graduate employability. This sub-project contributes to PDP-RM outcomes relating to STI capacity building, expansion of digital infrastructure in HEIs, and strengthened innovation ecosystems.

3. Open Theater and Business Center

This sub-project integrates cultural, entrepreneurial, and instructional development by constructing an open-field theater and a multi-purpose Business Center. The open theater, designed with bleachers and landscaped surroundings, provides a venue for academic events, cultural performances, campus-wide ceremonies, and outdoor learning activities. The Business Center will support student-led enterprises, BSBA experiential learning, university income-generating projects, and partnerships with industry stakeholders. These facilities promote creativity, innovation, and practical entrepreneurial skills, contributing to PDP-RM goals on improved lifelong learning systems, strengthened creative industries, institutional fiscal sustainability, and enhanced higher education performance outcomes.

The SMART-DIP TOC aligns with and contributes to the Philippine Development Plan (PDP) 2023–2028 Results Matrix by supporting key indicators under:

• Improved education quality and learning environments (Subchapter 2.2), including increased HEI performance in international rankings and improved licensure exam results;

• Advancement of research, development, and innovation (RDI) through the expansion of ICT capability and digital infrastructure (Chapter 9);

• Human capital development by providing equitable, modern, and technology-enabled learning spaces (Chapter 4);

• Cultural development and innovation ecosystems through the Open Theater and Business Center;

• Infrastructure expansion for resilient and inclusive growth (Chapter 10), especially through green, sustainable, and technology-integrated facility designs.

The construction of these government facilities is essential to ensuring that SLSU–Tomas Oppus Campus can:

• accommodate rapid enrollment growth;
• provide modernized and technology-enabled instruction;
• improve research and innovation productivity;
• develop entrepreneurial and creative competencies;
• meet national and regional standards for digital education and innovation readiness; and
• contribute meaningfully to regional socio-economic development and human capital formation.

These components jointly demonstrate clear responsiveness to PDP-RM priorities, justify government investment, and ensure that the campus meets its mandate as a developing hub for digital innovation and academic excellence in Southern Leyte.

The Establishment of the Women Center at MSU-Maguindanao is a flagship initiative designed to provide a holistic, trauma-informed, and gender-responsive facility for women and children in the Bangsamoro—particularly those affected by conflict, displacement, and gender-based violence. The Center will integrate essential services such as a Mental Health and Psychosocial Support (MHPSS) Clinic, trauma and healing rooms, private counseling spaces, breathing and meditation zones, and a Child Minding and Therapeutic Play Center. It also includes livelihood hubs, a women-focused sports and recreation area, and a Temporary Housing Facility that offers dignified shelter to survivors of violence or crisis. Beyond service delivery, the Center will function as a Women, Peace, and Security (WPS) Knowledge Repository and Journal Hub to strengthen research, policy dialogue, and community-based learning. By merging psychosocial care, protection mechanisms, economic empowerment, and knowledge production, the Women Center positions MSU-Maguindanao as a key actor in healing-centered peacebuilding and women’s empowerment in the region.

This project responds directly to the NAPWPS 2023–2033, particularly under:
•	Pillar I (Empowerment and Participation), by promoting community healing, economic self-reliance, and academic empowerment as foundations for women’s leadership and participation in peace processes and governance;
•	Pillar II (Protection and Prevention), by ensuring access to safe, gender-responsive environments—whether through psychosocial care, temporary shelter, or child-friendly facilities—for GBV survivors, displaced families, and former combatants;
•	Pillar III (Promotion and Mainstreaming), by modeling trauma-informed, gender-sensitive infrastructure that can be institutionalized in local development plans, DRRM strategies, and GAD programming—supporting peace education and civic engagement through the lens of care;
•	and Pillar IV (Monitoring, Evaluation, Accountability, and Learning), by enabling data-informed practices, documentation of recovery, and the generation of knowledge on women’s psychosocial experiences, economic recovery, and peacebuilding contributions.

The project aims to establish baseline information of the suitability of existing postharvest processing practices (fermentation and drying) and its impact to the quality of cacao beans, through chemical characterization cacao beans. The project also aims to provide an impact overview concerning soil health and its relation to yield quality and farm productivity. Specifically, the following will be addressed: 
a. Evaluate the effect of postharvest processing practices (fermentation and drying) on the quality of cacao beans supplies in the region.
b. To chemically characterized cacao beans associated with implemented postharvest processing practices;
c. Determine soil health of cacao farms and its impact to yield quality of cacao fruits and farm productivity;
d. Establish baseline information of factors affecting the quality of cacao bean supplies and farm productivity in the region.
